DTSTART:20261203T080500Z
DTEND:20261203T082500Z
SUMMARY:EPW 2026: Opening presentations
LOCATION:Auditorium\, GIZ Campus Forum\, Friedrich-Ebert-Allee 32\, 53113 B
 onn\, Germany
DESCRIPTION:Cross-regional findings from the Global Coalition for Energy Pl
 anning (GCEP) regional dialogues on the bottlenecks between planning and i
 nvestment\, and a perspective from GET.pro.\n\nProvisional programme\, che
 ck for changes: https://energyplanningweek.info/#opening-d3
URL:https://energyplanningweek.info/#opening-d3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:session-7@energyplanningweek.info
DTSTAMP:20261126T164119Z
DTSTART:20261203T083000Z
DTEND:20261203T100000Z
SUMMARY:EPW 2026: Session 7: From energy planning to investment decisions 
 – the case of power utilities
LOCATION:Auditorium\, GIZ Campus Forum\, Friedrich-Ebert-Allee 32\, 53113 B
 onn\, Germany
DESCRIPTION:Power-sector investment planning offers a concrete case in whic
 h scenario findings can guide specific investments\, particularly where st
 ate-owned or vertically integrated utilities can commit to them. Building 
 on initial findings from the GCEP Planning-to-Investment Dialogues\, this 
 session examines what planning requirements support the translation of lon
 g-term power-sector scenarios into investable plans\, how these differ acr
 oss investment arrangements – utility investment programmes\, public pro
 curement and private-sector investment – and how conditions such as util
 ity creditworthiness\, debt sustainability and financial risks can be cons
 idered during planning.\n\nProvisional programme\, check for changes: http
 s://energyplanningweek.info/#session-7
URL:https://energyplanningweek.info/#session-7
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:session-8@energyplanningweek.info
DTSTAMP:20261126T164119Z
DTSTART:20261203T103000Z
DTEND:20261203T120000Z
SUMMARY:EPW 2026: Session 8: Planning the transition away from fossil fuels
LOCATION:Auditorium\, GIZ Campus Forum\, Friedrich-Ebert-Allee 32\, 53113 B
 onn\, Germany
DESCRIPTION:The transition away from fossil fuels (TAFF) agenda through the
  lens of investment-ready energy planning: how national planning systems c
 an translate transition-away commitments into credible implementation path
 ways\, and what practical support GCEP can provide to facilitate dialogue 
 between planners\, policymakers and financiers on the conditions needed to
  move from political commitment to investable action. It also reflects on 
 the development and climate nexus.\n\nProvisional programme\, check for ch
 anges: https://energyplanningweek.info/#session-8
URL:https://energyplanningweek.info/#session-8
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lunch-d3@energyplanningweek.info
DTSTAMP:20261126T164119Z
DTSTART:20261203T120000Z
DTEND:20261203T133000Z
SUMMARY:EPW 2026: Lunch break and working lunch session: Planning for unive
 rsal access – turning national targets into a bankable last mile
LOCATION:Auditorium\, GIZ Campus Forum\, Friedrich-Ebert-Allee 32\, 53113 B
 onn\, Germany
DESCRIPTION:Lunch from 13:00\; the working lunch session runs from 13:00 to
  14:00. Many countries now hold a national strategy or least-cost plan for
  universal access\, yet turning it into a financed pipeline remains diffic
 ult\, particularly in Sub-Saharan Africa. This working lunch brings govern
 ment planners\, regulators\, data and tool providers\, and financiers toge
 ther in small groups around real country cases to examine where access pla
 ns stall on the way to investment and what would move them forward.\n\nPro
 visional programme\, check for changes: https://energyplanningweek.info/#l
 unch-d3
URL:https://energyplanningweek.info/#lunch-d3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:session-9@energyplanningweek.info
DTSTAMP:20261126T164119Z
DTSTART:20261203T133000Z
DTEND:20261203T144500Z
SUMMARY:EPW 2026: Session 9: From plans to delivery – how can planning su
 pport enabling energy markets?
LOCATION:Auditorium\, GIZ Campus Forum\, Friedrich-Ebert-Allee 32\, 53113 B
 onn\, Germany
DESCRIPTION:What it takes to turn energy planning priorities into commercia
 lly viable projects\, infrastructure and functioning markets. The session 
 looks at what needs to happen from planning to action\, from policy\, regu
 lation and procurement to market design and investment decisions\, and whe
 re key barriers can emerge along the way\, bringing together perspectives 
 from different actors on how governments and planning institutions can cre
 ate the conditions for investment and strengthen the link between long-ter
 m planning and delivery.\n\nProvisional programme\, check for changes: htt
 ps://energyplanningweek.info/#session-9
URL:https://energyplanningweek.info/#session-9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:session-10@energyplanningweek.info
DTSTAMP:20261126T164119Z
DTSTART:20261203T151500Z
DTEND:20261203T164500Z
SUMMARY:EPW 2026: Session 10: Country cases – when planning meets the mar
 ket
LOCATION:Auditorium\, GIZ Campus Forum\, Friedrich-Ebert-Allee 32\, 53113 B
 onn\, Germany
DESCRIPTION:Country case presentations from three angles. Planning enables 
 the market (large scale): how long-term planning can inform large-scale pr
 oject procurement and create a pipeline investors can respond to. The mark
 et moves faster than planning (distributed generation): what happens when 
 markets\, prosumers or new technologies such as battery storage move ahead
  of the assumptions in the plan\, and how market information\, investor be
 haviour\, technology costs and emerging business models should feed back i
 nto planning. Planning enables infrastructure investment: how grid infrast
 ructure enables functioning energy markets and private investment\, and ho
 w planning can provide the long-term visibility\, coordination and investm
 ent framework needed to develop it.\n\nProvisional programme\, check for c
 hanges: https://energyplanningweek.info/#session-10
URL:https://energyplanningweek.info/#session-10
END:VEVENT
